{
  "why": "The declared-verb verdict, written once and read by two implementations: the Rust law `artifact_app_laws::declared_verb_findings` (`🧪️tests/⚖️declared-verb-verdicts/🦀️.rs`, which every plugin surface's `assert_declared_verbs_honour_their_declarations` applies to its own probes) and the AJV twin (`📦️packages/🟦️typescript/🧪️tests/⚖️declared-verb-verdicts/🟦️.ts`, which states the same rules as JSON Schema). A probe is one declared verb dispatched on its app's boot example from every window kind that presents it (`windows`), each with its staged declared defaults (`staged`) and with two distinct values per perturbable declared argument (`arguments`). An outcome is `unreachable` (the dispatch infrastructure refused it before any handler ran), `refused` (the handler refused it by name) or `settled` (the lanes it published and what it changed). The rules: (a) a view or shell verb never writes or replaces the document from any window; (b) an agent-facing mutation never settles silently on every probe; (c) an agent- or chrome-facing verb never answers two distinct values of one declared argument identically (equal settles always count; equal refusals count only when every other declared argument carried a value, `othersSpecified`, and the staged invocation of that window was refused identically too); a destructive verb that settles writes the document or a user file; every declared agent or chrome verb bridges with its staged declared defaults (an input gesture's payload is the host's) and every declared verb is reachable from at least one window; an agent-facing verb run through the agent lane (`agent`, the MCP prepare phase at the catalog's address) writes the document exactly when the shell lane does and is not refused where the shell acts; (c) holds when some window tells the two values apart. An unbridged or unreachable verb reports that alone.",
